        <tr><td class="bold">Medium</td><td class="num">9.59</td><td class="num">9.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="bold">High</td><td class="num">9.43</td><td class="num">9.0</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="callout callout-warning" style="margin-top:16px;">
      <div class="callout-title">Observation</div>
      Traffic density does not meaningfully impact wait times (variation &lt;2%). This suggests queue delays are driven primarily by charger availability and session duration rather than external traffic conditions. High-traffic scenarios actually show the lowest average wait time (9.43 min), possibly because drivers arriving during high traffic are more price- or time-sensitive and select less congested chargers.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== SECTION 3: ENERGY & GRID LOAD ===== -->
<section id="energy-grid-load" class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">3</span>
    <h2>Energy Consumption &amp; Grid Load Patterns</h2>
  </div>
  <p class="section-intro">Assessing energy consumption patterns, station load profiles across time slots and days of the week, and the relationship between charging power levels and grid stress.</p>

  <div class="grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Grid Load by Time Slot</h3>
      <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="chartLoadTimeslot"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Energy &amp; Load by Day of Week</h3>
      <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="chartDayOfWeek"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Charging Power Level Profiles</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Power Level</th><th class="num">Sessions</th><th class="num">Avg Load (kW)</th><th class="num">Avg Energy (kWh)</th><th class="num">Avg Duration (min)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="bold">7 kW (Level 2)</td><td class="num">2,088</td><td class="num">53.56</td><td class="num">39.30</td><td class="num">338.8</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="bold">11 kW (Level 2+)</td><td class="num">2,124</td><td class="num">53.76</td><td class="num">39.55</td><td class="num">217.7</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="bold">22 kW (Level 3)</td><td class="num">2,055</td><td class="num">52.93</td><td class="num">39.01</td><td class="num">108.4</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="bold">50 kW (DC Fast)</td><td class="num">2,087</td><td class="num">52.44</td><td class="num">38.45</td><td class="num">48.2</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="callout callout-insight" style="margin-top:16px;">
      <div class="callout-title">Key Finding</div>
      Energy consumed per session is nearly identical across power levels (~39 kWh), but session duration varies 7x between 7 kW (339 min) and 50 kW (48 min). Higher-power chargers turn over vehicles dramatically faster, which directly impacts queue throughput. However, station load profiles remain similar across power levels, suggesting grid infrastructure can accommodate the faster charger mix.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Grid Load: Peak vs Off-Peak by Day of Week (kW)</h3>
    <div class="chart-container tall">
      <canvas id="chartLoadDow"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="callout callout-warning" style="margin-top:16px;">
      <div class="callout-title">Grid Stress Pattern</div>
      Peak loads average 84.1 kW&mdash;2.4x the off-peak average of 34.6 kW. This 143% load swing is the most significant grid management challenge. The ratio is consistent across all days of the week, indicating a structural pattern rather than weekday-specific behavior.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== SECTION 4: RENEWABLE & PRICING ===== -->
