        <thead>
          <tr><th>Weather</th><th>Off-Peak</th><th>Peak</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Clear</td>
            <td class="heat-low">39.7%</td>
            <td class="heat-high">29.1%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Cloudy</td>
            <td class="heat-low">38.8%</td>
            <td class="heat-high">29.0%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Rainy</td>
            <td class="heat-low">39.4%</td>
            <td class="heat-high">28.9%</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout callout-insight" style="margin-top:16px;">
        <div class="callout-title">Weather Impact</div>
        Weather conditions have minimal impact on renewable penetration (&lt;1% variation within each time slot). The time-of-day effect dominates: off-peak consistently delivers ~39% renewable share regardless of weather, while peak drops to ~29%. This indicates that renewable availability is primarily time-driven (likely solar generation windows) rather than weather-driven in this network.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Renewable Ratio by Location Type</h3>
      <div class="chart-container" style="height:260px;">
        <canvas id="chartRenewLocation"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Dynamic Pricing Overview</h3>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Metric</th><th class="num">Value</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Average Electricity Price</td><td class="num bold">9.96 units</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Price&ndash;Demand Correlation</td><td class="num">-0.012 (negligible)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Price&ndash;Renewable Correlation</td><td class="num">-0.005 (negligible)</td></tr>
